WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.080
Proposition forty-four sets a spending rule for private

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.140
nonprofit safety-net clinics.

3
00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.840
These clinics serve many low-income and uninsured patients.

4
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.440
Covered clinics would have to spend at least

5
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.000
ninety percent

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.020
of annual revenue on health care services,

7
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.580
leaving no more than ten percent for other

8
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:17.940
expenses.

9
00:00:18.227 --> 00:00:20.567
A clinic falling short would owe a penalty

10
00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:21.927
equal to the shortfall.

11
00:00:22.427 --> 00:00:24.447
It could recover that money by complying within

12
00:00:24.447 --> 00:00:25.247
five years.

13
00:00:26.007 --> 00:00:28.967
Temporary waivers would be available in some circumstances.

14
00:00:29.677 --> 00:00:31.657
A voter might want a larger share of

15
00:00:31.657 --> 00:00:34.857
clinic revenue devoted to services for patients, with

16
00:00:34.857 --> 00:00:37.277
reporting and consequences for falling short.

17
00:00:37.563 --> 00:00:39.843
Another voter could worry that a rigid limit

18
00:00:39.843 --> 00:00:41.963
constrains necessary support costs,

19
00:00:42.563 --> 00:00:45.923
adds fees, or makes some clinics financially fragile.

20
00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.630
Yes adopts the new spending rule;

21
00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.670
no leaves existing reporting and spending requirements in

22
00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:52.950
place.

23
00:00:53.497 --> 00:00:55.277
Visit our website for the fuller explanation,

24
00:00:55.997 --> 00:00:58.237
source links, and official voter guide.
